<p>You can print a document using an imposition arrangement to give you 
 the finishing you require. Trimming a printout into several pages reduces 
 print cost.<br/>
Depending on the printer you are using, this function may not be available.</p>
<h2>When Printing One Copy (Speed)</h2>
<p>For example, you print an A4 document of four pages. Two pages of the 
 document are printed on one sheet of A3. The A3 sheets can then be cut 
 in half and stacked to make an A4 document of four pages.</p>
<p>In this example, four prints are produced, but the print count is only 
 two.</p>
<h2>When Printing Plural Copies (Double)</h2>
<p>For example, you print two copies of an A4 document. Two pages of the 
 document are printed on one sheet of A3. The A3 sheet can then be cut 
 in half to make two copies of the A4 document.</p>
<p>In this example, two prints are produced, but the print count is only 
 one.</p>
<p>Speed: <img alt="" class="help-img" height="78" loading="lazy" src="~/images/help/BSp.gif" width="77"/> Double: <img alt="" class="help-img" height="77" loading="lazy" src="~/images/help/BDub.gif" width="77"/></p>
<ol>
<li><p>Open the printing preferences dialog box after 
 creating a document in an application.</p></li>
<li><p>Click the <span class="Term-Soft">Imposition</span> 
 menu of the <span class="Term-Soft">Detailed Settings</span> tab.</p></li>
<li><p>Select the imposition method from the <span class="Term-Soft">Imposition 
 (Speed):</span> or <span class="Term-Soft">Imposition (Double):</span> list.<br/>
 The icons show the available page flows of the imposition type selected. 
 </p></li>
<li><p>After making any other necessary settings, click 
 <span class="Term-OS">OK</span> to close the printing preferences dialog 
 box.</p></li>
<li><p>Start printing.</p></li>
</ol>
